<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Differentiating the Inverse Trigonometric Functions</title>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            Now that we have these inverse functions, we want to differentiate them. 
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Now that we have these inverse functions, we want to differentiate them. We will use the fact that we know the derivative of the forward functions and use implicit differentiation.
        </p>

        <example workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = \sin^{-1}(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        

    </paragraphs>
</page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Derivatives of Inverse Trigonometric Functions</title>

        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivatives of <m>f(x) = \tan^{-1}(x)</m> and <m>g(x) = \sec^{-1}(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        

        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    
    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            What can we do for the other trigonometric functions?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What can we do for the other trigonometric functions? We have cofunction identities! That means these all just get minus signs.
        </p>


    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = e^x\tan^{-1}(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>

        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = \sin^{-1}(x^3)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative for each of the functions below.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = x^2\sin^{-1}(4x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \ln(\cos(x))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle h(x) = \sin(x)\sec^{-1}(e^{2x})</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative for each of the functions below.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \tan^{-1}(3x^2 + 5)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \ln\left(\frac{x^2 + 5}{(x^3 - 2x + 1)^2}\right)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle h(x) = x\sec^{-1}(2^x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 10/23</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Explore applications of derivatives in nature
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve motion problems using the derivative.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Derivatives in Nature</title>

<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    When thinking about applications of the derivative to physical/real-world problems, the overarching theme is that the derivative is the rate of change of a function.
</p>
<p component="a">
    When thinking about applications of the derivative to physical/real-world problems, the overarching theme is that the derivative is the rate of change of a function. Anything that is changing can be modeled with a function, and then its rate of change can be calculated using the derivative. If you want to know how fast something is changing, use the derivative/slope.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Motion Problems</title>

            <example>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Assume that the position of a remote-controlled car is given by <md>
                            s(t) = t^3 - \frac{23}{2}t^2 + 30 t
                        </md> over the time interval <m>[0,10]</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Where is the car at <m>t=0</m>?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Where is the car at <m>t=2</m>?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Find the velocity of the car at <m>t=1</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            When is the car at rest? Moving forward?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            How would you compute the total distance traveled over the interval <m>[0,10]</m>?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            What is the acceleration at <m>t=3</m>?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Graph the position, velocity, and acceleration.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task workspace="1in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            When is the car speeding up? Slowing down?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </example>
            
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            This type of information can also be learned directly from a graph of either position or velocity.
        </p>

        <example>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider a particle whose <em>position</em> is given by the graph below.
                </p>
                <image width="60%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-nature-ex2-position-graph">
                <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-nature-ex2-position-graph.xml" />
            </prefigure>
            </image>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle moving forward?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle moving backwards?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle moving at rest?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle speeding up?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle slowing down?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider a particle whose <em>velocity</em> is given by the graph below.
                </p>
                <image width="60%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-nature-ex2-velocity-graph">
                <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-nature-ex2-velocity-graph.xml" />
            </prefigure>
            </image>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle moving forward?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle moving backwards?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle moving at rest?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle speeding up?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When is the particle slowing down?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    A ball is thrown upward from the top of a building. The position of the ball is given by 
                    <md>
                        s(t) = -16t^2 + 40t + 10
                    </md> where <m>s</m> is in feet above the ground and <m>t</m> is in seconds.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        When does the ball reach its maximum height, and what is this height?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How fast is the ball moving when it hits the ground?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Other Applications</title>
            
            <p>
                There are many more applications to a variety of fields, and the book has many of them in it. As always, the theme is that the derivative is used to represent a rate of change.
            </p>

            <example workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>T(x)</m> be a function that represents the temperature of a metal rod <m>x</m> inches from the end. What does <m>T'(x)</m> represent?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            
            </example>

            <example workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>V(r)</m> be the volume of a sphere as a function of its radius. Compute <m>V'(r)</m> and explain the physical interpretation of <m>V'(4)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            
            </example>
            
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Assume that for two subjects that you're trying to learn about, <m>R_1(t)</m> and <m>R_2(t)</m> represents the fraction of the material you remember <m>t</m> days after last studying it. If <md>
                        R'_1(2) = -0.3 \qquad \text{and} \qquad R'_2(2) = -0.01 
                    </md>, which of the two classes should you be more concerned about? What do these derivatives represent physically?
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
</handout>


</section>